I consider myself fairly experienced with using LaTeX, and I wrote a "Lyx2LaTeX" script that automatically converts text written in LyX into LaTeX code. I write many papers for publications, and use LyX so much the compiler inserts ~~~~\whatevers where it expects a LaTeX command. I noticed this no longer happens when using the script, and I think I'll clean it up from time to time and/or put it in GitHub. Below, we'll see what the script does.

The script first "munges" my text, changing both sequence and style. It will rearrange the text into headings and paragraphs. At least, I think you can get this script to change everything to headings and paragraphs. The script uses the "combined" LaTeX class that comes with LyX. This requires a bit of tweaking to get it to work right, and I'll publish that in a later post. However, for now, we just run it.

We'll run the script on the first part of Morgan's Law \begin{align}\end{align}, which runs as text in my computer. Instead, the script runs a LaTeX command \begin{align}. We begin a new line with a backslash (\bfseries). You can see how the line ends with $$. This LaTeX command requires two vertices. After placing each of these, the munging is complete. The rest of the document examines the results, and marks this a "section". By the end of the document, a section heading is made with the "section" \part{First} command. The "section" is replaced with the heading of the first paragraph. Voila, a section.

